3	Malaria self-diagnosis and why it is dangerous	malaria-self-diagnosis-and-why-it-is-dangerous	Self-diagnosing malaria is one of the most common and dangerous health habits in Nigeria. Here is why it is a problem and what to do instead.	In Nigeria, it is common practice to feel unwell, assume it is malaria and head straight to a pharmacy for antimalarial medication. It happens every day. Most people do not think twice about it. But this habit, as familiar as it is, carries real risks that are worth understanding.\n\n**The clinical problem with presumptive treatment**\n\nThe typical Nigerian response to fever goes something like this. You feel hot, tired and achy. You recognise the feeling. You go to a pharmacy, describe your symptoms and walk out with antimalarial medication. Sometimes you feel better. You conclude it was malaria. The cycle repeats.\n\n**What this process is missing is a single, critical step. A test.**\n\nMalaria has no symptoms that belong exclusively to it. Fever, chills, headache, body aches, fatigue and nausea are also the presenting symptoms of typhoid fever, urinary tract infections, viral infections, severe anaemia, meningitis and a range of other conditions, some of which are significantly more dangerous than malaria and require completely different treatment.\n\nWhen you treat a fever as malaria without testing, you are not treating a diagnosis. You are treating a guess. And in medicine, guessing is not a strategy.\n\n**How easy access to antimalarials is making malaria harder to treat**\n\nPart of what makes self-diagnosis so embedded in Nigerian culture is how easy it is to access antimalarial drugs. Walk into any pharmacy in Lagos and you can buy them without a prescription, without a consultation and without any evidence that you actually have malaria.\n\nThis accessibility feels like a convenience. In reality it is contributing to one of the most serious public health challenges in the country: antimalarial resistance.\n\nMalaria parasites are not static. They evolve. And every time antimalarial medication is taken unnecessarily, it creates an environment where resistant strains are more likely to survive and spread. The drugs that have saved millions of lives across Africa are becoming less effective, and our habit of treating malaria without confirming it is one of the reasons why.\n\nThis is not a theoretical concern. Resistance to artemisinin-based combination therapies, the current frontline treatment for malaria in Nigeria, has already been documented in parts of Africa. The window to change this habit is narrowing.\n\n\n![blogpost-3 second-img.png](http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/44fb211d-065c-4a9c-a389-1867e8e31aa9.png)\n\n\n\n**The limits of self-assessment**\n\nThere is a phrase that comes up constantly when this conversation happens. I know my body. It is said with conviction and it is not entirely wrong. People do know their bodies. But knowing your body and being able to accurately diagnose what is happening inside it are two very different things.\n\nThe liver does not send you a signal when it is under strain. The kidneys do not announce when their function is declining. And the malaria parasite does not feel different from the dozens of other pathogens that produce the same set of symptoms. Familiarity with how illness feels is not the same as knowing what is causing it.\n\nDoctors spend years learning to distinguish between conditions that present similarly. Laboratory tests exist precisely because symptoms alone are not sufficient to make an accurate diagnosis. Trusting your instincts is valuable in many areas of life. Diagnosing infectious disease is not one of them.\n\n**What happens when the wrong condition goes untreated**\n\nBeyond the public health implications, self-diagnosing malaria carries very real personal costs.\n\nIf you do not have malaria and you treat it as if you do, the condition you actually have continues to progress. Typhoid left untreated can lead to intestinal perforation. A UTI that spreads to the kidneys becomes a much more serious problem. Meningitis that is mistaken for a bad fever can become life-threatening within hours.\n\nAntimalarial drugs also have side effects. Some affect vision. Some affect the heart rhythm. Taking them without a confirmed diagnosis means accepting those risks with no benefit on the other side.\n\nAnd then there is the cost of getting it wrong twice. You treat, you do not get better, you treat again. By the time you eventually see a doctor, the condition has had days or weeks to develop. What might have been a straightforward treatment has become a complicated one.\n\n**The case for testing before treating**\n\nTesting for malaria before treating it is not a radical idea. A rapid diagnostic test takes fifteen minutes and costs very little. A blood smear at a laboratory takes slightly longer but gives a definitive answer. Either option is faster than the time most people spend deciding which antimalarial to buy.\n\nThe argument against testing is usually convenience. But the convenience of skipping a test is a false economy when the cost of being wrong is your health.\n\n**What needs to change and why it matters**\n\nNigeria's relationship with self-medication is not unique. It is the product of a healthcare system that has historically been difficult to access, expensive and overstretched. When seeing a doctor feels like a significant undertaking, people find workarounds. The pharmacy becomes the first point of care. Familiar symptoms get familiar treatments.\n\nBut this workaround, however understandable its origins, is costing us. In drug efficacy, in delayed diagnoses and in lives.\n\nThe next time the fever comes, get tested first. Not because you do not know your body. But because your body deserves more than a guess.\n	http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/a7e36295-f75a-4cf7-b1d2-8c50a867561f.png	Malaria self-diagnosis and why it is dangerous	Self-diagnosing malaria is one of the most common and dangerous health habits in Nigeria. Here is why it is a problem and what to do instead.	t	2026-06-21 17:11:49.722785+01	2026-06-21 17:11:31.661178+01	2026-06-21 17:24:55.642783+01		GENERAL_HEALTH
4	What every employer should know before hiring domestic staff	what-every-employer-should-know-before-hiring-domestic-staff	 Before hiring a driver, nanny or cook, a proper health screening is essential. What to check, why it matters and how to get it done at Grover's Hospital Lagos.\n	Hiring domestic staff is a decision most Lagos households make regularly. A driver, a nanny, a cook, a security guard. The process usually involves a referral, a conversation and a start date. What it often does not involve is a health screening. That is the gap this article addresses.\n\nThe absence of a health screening in the domestic hiring process is not usually a deliberate choice. It is more often an oversight, something that feels unnecessary when someone comes with a strong recommendation or has worked for a family friend. But a referral is not a health check. And the two should not be confused.\n\n**Why a health screening matters before someone starts**\n\nDomestic workers spend significant time in your home and around your family. A nanny has direct, sustained contact with your children. A cook handles food that everyone in the household eats. A driver is in close proximity to family members on a daily basis. A security guard is present on the property around the clock.\n\nIn each of these roles, the health status of the person you are hiring is directly relevant to the health and safety of your household. This is not an abstract concern. Communicable diseases, infectious conditions and certain chronic illnesses that go undetected can pose real risks in a close-contact domestic environment.\n\nA screening before they start is not about distrust. It is not a statement about the individual's character or reliability. It is about having the information you need to make a responsible hiring decision, the same way a corporate employer runs a background check before onboarding a new employee.\n\n**What does the screening actually involve?**\n\nA domestic staff health screening is not a single test. It is a panel of assessments that checks for the conditions most relevant to a household employment setting. The five most important tests in this context are:\n\n- Hepatitis B and Hepatitis C Both are viral infections that affect the liver. Hepatitis B in particular is highly transmissible and can be passed through close contact, shared items or minor cuts and abrasions. Screening for both is a standard component of any responsible domestic staff health check.\n\n- Tuberculosis TB remains a significant public health concern in Nigeria and is transmissible through respiratory contact. A serum TB test is included in most domestic staff screening tiers. In more comprehensive packages, sputum microscopy provides additional confirmation.\n\n- H. pylori This bacterial infection affects the gastrointestinal system and is particularly relevant for anyone involved in food preparation. It can be transmitted through food and water and often goes undetected without a specific test.\n\n- Malaria parasite testing Active malaria infection affects an individual's ability to work effectively and can be transmitted through mosquito vectors in a shared living environment. Testing for malaria as part of a pre-employment screen is standard practice.\n\n- Full blood count A CBC gives a broad picture of general health, immune function and any underlying conditions that may not be immediately apparent. It is one of the most informative single tests in any screening panel.\n\nBeyond these five, a well-structured screening will typically also include urinalysis and where relevant, a pregnancy test and abdomino-pelvic scan.\n\n**How comprehensive should the screening be?**\n\nThe answer depends on the nature of the role. For a driver or security guard, the core tests covering communicable diseases and general health status are usually sufficient. For a nanny, a cook or anyone with close daily contact with children, the elderly or anyone with a compromised immune system, a more thorough panel is warranted. The closer the contact and the more vulnerable the household members, the more comprehensive the screening should be.\n\n**What happens with the results?**\n\nResults from a standard domestic staff screening are typically available within 24 to 48 hours. A doctor reviews the findings and you receive a clear picture of the individual's current health status.\n\nIt is important to understand that a health screening is not a pass or fail process in the traditional sense. The results give you information. What you do with that information requires judgement and, where relevant, a conversation with the individual concerned. A result that requires attention does not automatically disqualify someone from employment. It opens a conversation about what steps, if any, are needed before they start.\n\nSome findings may require follow-up testing or treatment before employment begins. Others may be managed conditions that have no bearing on the individual's ability to perform the role safely. The doctor reviewing the results will be in a position to advise on what is and is not clinically significant.\n\n**When should the screening happen?**\n\nIdeally before the person starts work, not after. Getting the screening done before a trial period begins gives both parties clarity from the outset and avoids the more difficult conversation of addressing a health finding after someone has already been working in your home.\n\nIn practice, there is sometimes pressure to move quickly, particularly when a household urgently needs cover. In those situations, it is worth making the health screening one of the first steps in the process rather than something to be addressed later. A turnaround of 24 to 48 hours means it does not have to be a significant delay.\n\nIt is also worth noting that a screening has a limited validity window. The results reflect the individual's health status at the time the tests were conducted. For long-term domestic employees, periodic rescreening once every one to two years is a sensible practice, particularly for conditions like TB where ongoing exposure is a factor.\n\n\n![blogpost4-body.png](http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/a4b71e79-7e10-47d5-851b-6102a6fab1e0.png)\n\n\n**What responsible hiring looks like**\n\nThe households that handle this well are the ones that treat domestic staff screening as a standard, non-negotiable step in the hiring process rather than something triggered by a specific concern. When it is presented that way, it normalises the process for both employer and employee, removes any stigma associated with it and ensures the process is handled in a structured, professional setting.\n\nIt also creates a record. If a health concern arises during the course of employment, having a baseline screening on file is valuable. It establishes what the individual's health status was at the point of hiring and provides context for any subsequent developments.\n\nThe conversation around domestic staff health screening in Nigeria is still developing. In many households it remains optional, informal or absent entirely. But as awareness of occupational health standards grows and as households become more informed about their responsibilities as employers, health screening is increasingly becoming the norm rather than the exception.\n\n**A note on confidentiality**\n\nHealth information obtained during a pre-employment screening is confidential. It should not be shared beyond the individuals directly involved in the hiring decision and should be stored securely. This applies whether the screening is conducted at a hospital, a clinic or any other medical facility.\n\nIf a health finding is relevant to the hiring decision, the appropriate course of action is to discuss it with the individual directly, with discretion and without unnecessary disclosure to third parties. Treating health information with the same level of care as other sensitive personal information is both an ethical obligation and a matter of basic professional conduct.\n\nA health screening is a small step in what is ultimately a relationship built on trust. It does not replace the referral, the conversation or the trial period. It complements them. It adds a layer of information that is objective, clinical and genuinely useful, both for the household making the hiring decision and for the individual being hired, who may learn something about their own health in the process.\n\nDone well, it protects everyone involved. And in a household setting, that is exactly what it should do.\n\n	http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/6f10b228-e909-42ab-b8c9-1833ba2e69a2.png	What every employer should know before hiring domestic staff	 Before hiring a driver, nanny or cook, a proper health screening is essential. What to check, why it matters and how to get it done at Grover's Hospital Lagos.\n	t	2026-06-21 17:41:01.926338+01	2026-06-21 17:40:57.533125+01	2026-06-21 17:41:01.935341+01		SCREENING_AND_PACKAGES
5	The complete guide to annual health checks	the-complete-guide-to-annual-health-checks	What does an annual health check actually cover? Everything you need to know about the wellness test at Grover's Hospital Victoria Island Lagos and how to choose the right tier.\n	Most people only think about their health when something goes wrong. A persistent cough, an unusual pain, a result that comes back abnormal. By that point, whatever is happening in the body has usually been developing for some time. An annual health check is built around a different philosophy entirely. It is not about responding to illness. It is about staying ahead of it.\n\nDespite this, annual health checks remain underutilised in Nigeria. Many people are aware they should probably have one. Far fewer actually do. This guide covers what an annual health check involves, why it matters and how to approach it practically.\n\n**What is an annual health check?**\n\nAn annual health check, also called a wellness test, is a comprehensive health screening designed to give you a full picture of where your health stands at a given point in time. Unlike a visit to the doctor prompted by a specific symptom, a wellness test is proactive. It is not looking for a single condition. It is assessing multiple systems and markers simultaneously to identify anything that warrants attention before it becomes a problem.\n\nThe scope of a wellness test goes well beyond what most people would get from a routine GP consultation. It covers blood chemistry, organ function, cardiovascular markers, imaging, infectious disease screening and in higher tiers, cancer risk markers, thyroid function and advanced cardiac imaging.\n\n**Why once a year?**\n\nThe annual cadence is not arbitrary. Many of the conditions that an annual health check screens for develop gradually over months and years. Hypertension, diabetes, elevated cholesterol, early-stage kidney disease and thyroid dysfunction are all conditions that can be present for a significant period before producing symptoms. Checking once a year gives you a consistent baseline and makes it possible to identify trends over time, which is often more informative than a single result in isolation.\n\nFor some people, depending on age, family history and existing health conditions, more frequent monitoring of specific markers may be appropriate. But for most healthy adults, an annual check provides the right balance of vigilance and practicality.\n\n**What does it actually cover?**\n\nA well-structured annual health check covers several distinct areas of health. Here is what each one tells you and why it matters:\n\n- Diabetic profile Fasting blood sugar and glycated haemoglobin (HBA1c) assess your blood sugar control. HBA1c in particular reflects your average blood sugar levels over the past two to three months, making it a more reliable indicator of diabetes risk than a single fasting reading.\n\n- Lipid profile Triglycerides, total cholesterol, HDL, LDL and VRDL cholesterol together give you a detailed picture of your cardiovascular risk. Elevated cholesterol is one of the primary drivers of heart disease and stroke, and most people with high cholesterol have no symptoms whatsoever.\n\n- Renal profile EUCR (electrolytes, urea and creatinine) and calcium levels assess how well your kidneys are functioning. Kidney disease often develops silently and a renal profile is one of the most important components of any annual screen, particularly for individuals with hypertension or diabetes.\n\n- Liver profile A full liver function panel covering SGOT, SGPT, alkaline phosphatase, GGT, bilirubin and proteins assesses liver health. Given the prevalence of Hepatitis B in Nigeria and the impact of diet and alcohol consumption on liver function, this is a critical component of the annual check.\n\n- Physical examination A full physical examination and clinical review by a doctor is the foundation of the entire process. It contextualises the results, identifies anything that requires follow-up and gives you the opportunity to discuss your health history and any concerns directly with a medical professional.\n\nBeyond these five, a comprehensive annual check will also include a thyroid panel, cancer risk markers, cardiac imaging, immunology screening and general tests depending on the tier selected.\n\n**How comprehensive should your check be?**\nThe honest answer is that it depends on where you are in life and what your risk profile looks like. A younger adult with no family history of chronic disease needs a different level of screening than someone in their mid-forties managing hypertension. If you are unsure where to start, a conversation with a doctor before booking is the most practical first step. They can point you in the right direction based on your specific situation.\n\n\n![blogpost5-body.png](http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/0bf9c19d-93b2-4011-8ffa-26fb3b9803aa.png)\n\n\n**Preparing for your annual health check**\n\nA few practical points before you go:\n\nFast for at least eight hours before the appointment. Several of the blood tests, including fasting blood sugar and lipid profile, require a fasting sample to produce accurate results. Water is fine to drink.\n\nBring a list of any medications you are currently taking. Some medications can affect certain test results and the doctor reviewing your results needs to know what you are on.\n\nBe ready to give a urine and stool sample. These are part of the standard panel and the laboratory will provide the necessary containers.\n\nWear comfortable clothing. The physical examination and ECG require access to the chest and limbs, so loose or layered clothing makes the process easier.\n\nAllow enough time. A comprehensive wellness test is not a fifteen-minute appointment. Depending on the tier, plan for a couple of hours at the facility.\n\n**Understanding your results**\n\nResults from most components of the annual health check are available within 24 to 48 hours. Imaging results such as ultrasound and chest X-ray are typically available on the same day or the day after.\n\nWhen you receive your results, it is worth reviewing them with a doctor rather than interpreting them in isolation. Reference ranges provide a useful guide but individual results need to be considered in the context of your overall health picture, your age, your medications and your history. A result that falls slightly outside the reference range is not automatically cause for concern. Equally, a result within range does not always mean there is nothing to address.\n\nThe goal is not to generate anxiety. It is to generate information. And information, in the right hands, is what makes early intervention possible.\n\n**The value of a consistent baseline**\n\nOne of the most underappreciated benefits of doing an annual health check every year is the baseline it creates. A single set of results tells you where you are today. A set of results taken year on year tells you whether things are stable, improving or moving in a direction that warrants attention.\n\nThis longitudinal picture is something that a one-off visit to a doctor prompted by a specific symptom cannot provide. It is one of the strongest arguments for making the annual health check a consistent habit rather than something you do once and then forget about.\n\n**Who should prioritize this**\n\nEveryone benefits from an annual health check. But there are groups for whom it is particularly important:\n\nAnyone over 35, regardless of how healthy they feel. Adults with a family history of heart disease, diabetes, hypertension or cancer. People managing chronic conditions such as hypertension, diabetes or kidney disease. Anyone who has not had a comprehensive health check in more than two years. Corporate employees and professionals whose lifestyle involves high stress, irregular meals and limited physical activity.\n\nIf you fall into any of these categories and you have not had a wellness test recently, this is the most actionable thing you can do for your long-term health right now.\n\n**Prevention as a practice**\n\nThe healthcare system in Nigeria, like many others, is structured primarily around treatment. You develop a condition, you seek care, you receive treatment. The annual health check sits outside this model entirely. It is not triggered by illness. It is not reactive. It is a deliberate, informed investment in understanding your own health before anything goes wrong.\n\nThat shift in approach, from reactive to proactive, is one of the most significant things an individual can do to protect their long-term health. It does not require a dramatic lifestyle change. It requires one appointment, once a year.\n	http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/1c8149e2-dacd-4cbf-818e-262059fa5806.png	The complete guide to annual health checks	What does an annual health check actually cover? Everything you need to know about the wellness test at Grover's Hospital Victoria Island Lagos and how to choose the right tier.\n	t	2026-06-21 18:05:42.382466+01	2026-06-21 18:05:40.042699+01	2026-06-21 18:05:42.389471+01		SCREENING_AND_PACKAGES
2	Everything you need to know before your pre-employment medical test	everything-you-need-to-know-before-your-pre-employment-medical-test	 What happens during a pre-employment medical test, why employers require it and what to expect on the day. A complete guide from Grover's Hospital Lagos.	If you have ever been told that a job offer is conditional on passing a medical test, you are not alone. Pre-employment medical tests are now a standard part of the hiring process across many industries in Nigeria. But most people arrive at the appointment with very little idea of what is actually going to happen. This guide covers everything you need to know.\n\n**Why do employers require a pre-employment medical test?**\n\nA pre-employment medical test exists to confirm that a new employee is physically fit to carry out the responsibilities of their role. For the employer, it is a form of due diligence. For the employee, it is an opportunity to understand their own health status, often for the first time in a while.\n\nIn some industries, medical clearance is a regulatory requirement. In others, it is simply good practice. Either way, the test is not designed to catch people out. It is designed to give both parties a clear and honest picture of where things stand before the employment relationship begins.\n\n**What does the test actually involve?**\n\nA pre-employment medical test is not a single test. It is a panel of screenings conducted in a single visit, usually by a doctor or a trained medical team. What is included depends on the employer's requirements and the nature of the role, but the most common tests are:\n\n**A general medical consultation and physical examination**\nA doctor reviews your medical history, takes your vitals and conducts a physical examination. This is the foundation of the assessment.\n\n**Blood tests** \nThese typically include HIV screening, Hepatitis B and C, genotype, blood group and blood sugar. For roles with higher health and safety requirements, a full blood count and lipid profile may also be included.\n\n**Chest X-ray** \nA chest X-ray is almost always part of a pre-employment screen. It checks for tuberculosis, which remains a significant public health concern in Nigeria, as well as other respiratory conditions.\n\n**Urinalysis** \nA urine test checks kidney function and can identify infections or other abnormalities.\n\n**Stool microscopy** \nThis is particularly relevant for roles involving food handling or close contact with vulnerable populations. It checks for parasitic infections and gastrointestinal conditions.\n\nFor more senior roles or those with specific physical demands, additional tests such as an ECG, a visual acuity test or a toxicology screen may be required.\n\n**What happens at the end?**\nOnce all the tests are completed and the results are reviewed by a doctor, a Fit-to-Work Form is issued. This is the document your employer receives as confirmation of your medical clearance. If any results require attention, the doctor will discuss this with you directly.\n\nIt is worth noting that a Fit-to-Work Form does not mean a person is in perfect health. It means they are medically fit to perform the duties of the role they have been hired for. The two are not the same thing.\n\n**What should you do to prepare?**\n\nFor most pre-employment tests, very little preparation is needed. However there are a few things worth knowing:\n\n- Go on an empty stomach if possible. Some blood tests, particularly blood sugar, require a fasting sample. Arriving without having eaten for at least 8 hours ensures your results are accurate.\n- Bring any relevant medical history. If you have a known condition or are on regular medication, let the doctor know at the start of the consultation. This context helps the doctor interpret your results correctly.\n- Drink water. Being well hydrated makes blood draws easier and produces a better urine sample.\n- Arrive early. The process involves multiple steps and takes time. Rushing through it does not serve anyone well.\n\n**Can you fail a pre-employment medical test?**\n\nThis is one of the most common questions people have going into the test and the answer is more nuanced than a simple yes or no.\n\nA pre-employment medical test is not a pass or fail examination in the traditional sense. The results are reviewed in the context of the specific role. A condition that would be relevant for a physically demanding role may have no bearing on a desk-based position. The doctor issuing the Fit-to-Work Form makes a professional judgement based on what the role requires.\n\nWhat the test can do is reveal health conditions you were not previously aware of. In that sense, regardless of the employment outcome, the information is valuable. Knowing your health status gives you the opportunity to act on it.\n\n**Conclusion**\n\nA pre-employment medical test is a small moment in what is hopefully a long and healthy working life. But it is also a useful one. It gives your employer the confidence they need and gives you information about your own health that is worth having regardless of the job.\n\nIf you are going in for one, go prepared, go honest and go knowing that the process exists to protect everyone involved, including you.\n	http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/312ba2f7-e5ae-44bc-897d-52bf190de515.png	Everything you need to know before your pre-employment medical test	 What happens during a pre-employment medical test, why employers require it and what to expect on the day. A complete guide from Grover's Hospital Lagos.	t	2026-06-20 17:38:51.053498+01	2026-06-20 16:14:54.215955+01	2026-06-21 18:11:34.788565+01	medical-tests	SCREENING_AND_PACKAGES
6	High blood pressure and why most people do not know they have it	high-blood-pressure-and-why-most-people-do-not-know-they-have-it	 High blood pressure affects millions of Nigerians silently. Learn what causes it, how it is diagnosed and how to manage it. A guide from Grover's Hospital Lagos.\n	If you asked most people in Lagos whether they have high blood pressure, the majority would say no. Not because they have been tested and confirmed it. But because they feel fine. And feeling fine, in the Nigerian health consciousness, is broadly understood to mean nothing is wrong.\n\nThis is the most dangerous assumption in modern healthcare.\n\n**The silent progression of hypertension**\n\nHigh blood pressure, or hypertension, does not hurt. It does not keep you awake at night. It does not stop you from going to work, exercising or eating well. In its early and even moderate stages, it produces no symptoms that most people would recognize as a reason to see a doctor.\n\nWhat it does instead is work quietly in the background, putting consistent strain on your heart, blood vessels, kidneys and brain over months and years. By the time it makes itself known, through a heart attack, a stroke, kidney failure or vision loss, the damage has been accumulating for a long time.\n\nThis is not a rare or extreme scenario. It is the most common trajectory of untreated hypertension. And in Nigeria, where routine health checks are not yet a cultural norm, it plays out daily.\n\n**Hypertension in Nigeria by the numbers**\n\nResearch on hypertension prevalence in Lagos has found rates significantly higher than global averages. Studies have documented hypertension prevalence of over 50% among adults in Lagos, compared to global averages closer to 30%. More concerning is that a substantial proportion of those individuals are unaware of their condition.\n\nThis is not primarily a story about access to treatment. It is a story about a gap between how many people have high blood pressure and how many people know they have it. That gap is where the real danger lives.\n\n**Why urban professionals are at higher risk**\n\nHypertension does not develop in a vacuum. It is shaped by the environment we live in and the lives we lead. And for many Lagos professionals, that environment is a near-perfect storm of risk factors.\n\nThe diet is a significant contributor. Processed foods, high sodium content, irregular eating patterns and the prevalence of convenience food across the city all put consistent pressure on the cardiovascular system. Physical inactivity compounds this. Lagos is not a city built for walking. Long commutes, desk-based work and the sheer exhaustion of navigating the city leave very little time or energy for regular exercise.\n\nThen there is stress. Chronic, sustained stress raises blood pressure directly. It drives behaviors like poor sleep, excessive alcohol consumption and smoking that raise it further. And it is embedded in the fabric of professional life in Lagos in a way that is rarely acknowledged as the health risk it actually is.\n\nNone of these factors in isolation are enough to cause hypertension. Together, over years, they create the conditions for it.\n\n\n![blogpost2-body.png](http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/af3b51e7-99dd-4305-b2e7-7769ba3cca8d.png)\n \n\n**The danger of symptom-based self-assessment**\n\nThere is a deeply held belief in Nigerian culture that the body will tell you when something is wrong. That if you were seriously ill, you would know it. This belief is understandable. It is also medically incorrect.\n\nThe body is remarkably good at adapting. It compensates for damage, maintains function under strain and often continues to perform normally long after a problem has taken hold. High blood pressure is the clearest example of this. The cardiovascular system can sustain elevated pressure for years without producing symptoms that register as a warning sign.\n\nFeeling fine is not a health status. It is the absence of obvious symptoms. Those are two very different things. And building a health strategy around the former is a significant risk.\n\n**What actually needs to change**\n\nThe solution to Nigeria's hypertension problem is not complicated at the individual level. It is a blood pressure reading. A single measurement taken during a routine consultation can identify elevated blood pressure before it has caused significant damage. At that point, the options for management are broad. Lifestyle changes alone, reducing salt, losing weight, exercising more regularly and managing stress, can be enough to bring blood pressure back to a healthy level for many people. For others, medication is needed. Either way, the earlier the intervention, the better the outcome.\n\nWhat needs to change is the assumption that a health check is something you do when you are unwell. The entire point of a health check is that you do it before anything goes wrong. That shift in mindset, from reactive to proactive healthcare, is arguably the most important health decision a Lagos professional can make.\n\n**A call for proactive healthcare**\n\nHigh blood pressure is not a condition that happens to unhealthy people. It happens to busy people, stressed people, people who are too focused on everything else to pay attention to their own health. It happens to people who feel fine.\n\nThe most effective thing you can do for your long-term health right now is not a dramatic lifestyle overhaul. It is knowing your numbers. Blood pressure, blood sugar, cholesterol. These three markers tell you more about your cardiovascular risk than almost anything else. And none of them require you to feel unwell to find out.\n\nThe conversation about hypertension in Nigeria needs to move from treatment to prevention. From the hospital to the annual check-up. From crisis management to informed decision-making.\n\nThat shift starts with a single appointment.\n	http://localhost:8080/api/v1/files/690d00ce-2fa2-4962-aeb3-8a534601a70f.png	High blood pressure and why most people do not know they have it	 High blood pressure affects millions of Nigerians silently. Learn what causes it, how it is diagnosed and how to manage it. A guide from Grover's Hospital Lagos.\n	t	2026-06-21 18:18:19.287752+01	2026-06-21 18:18:04.267772+01	2026-06-21 18:18:19.295757+01		LIFESTYLE_DISEASES
